  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 349 AM MDT FRI OCT 9 2026\nCome Monday, there remains considerable guidance uncertainty over \nthe final track of the closed upper low as it either shifts slightly \neastward directly over our area into the upper Great Basin or \nimmediately dig further down into southern California. For now, the \nbulk of the advected moisture from the projected remnant of Tropical \nStorm Rachel will steer further northeast over the Upper Rockies by \nlate Monday. This moisture track will keep moderate to high \nprecipitation chances (30 to 60%) mainly over the ID side as it \ngradually tapers off east on Monday, with the highest chances over \nhigh terrain areas. Monday will continue seeing temperatures 5 to 10 \ndegrees below normal with the low passage. The low passage will keep \nsnow levels in the 4000-5000 ft MSL range on the eastern OR side, \nand southerly flow ahead of the approaching low will keep snow \nlevels at 7500-9000 ft MSL on the southwest/west-central ID side, \nkeeping the bulk of the precipitation as rain, with some light \nsnow/wintry mix over high mountain peaks. This pattern is subject to \na slight change, as the final track of the upper low remains \nuncertain. \n\nAs this system pushes out east, Tuesday will see around a \n20-40% chance of precipitation over central and south-central ID. \nCome Wednesday, there is general guidance agreement over a shortwave \ntrough digging north of us from British Columbia, possibly bringing \nsome precip over the northernmost mountain areas of our CWA late \nWednesday through early Thursday. Otherwise, a highly amplified \nPacific ridge will build in and return dry conditions and a slight \nwarming trend Thursday through Friday. Overall, near-normal seasonal \ntemperatures will be expected throughout the long-term period.",
